    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">59 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> establish Florentia as a colony for veteran soldiers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">285</span><span class="rf-text">Florence becomes the capital of the Tuscia region</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1115</span><span class="rf-text">The city is governed by an autonomous commune</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1252</span><span class="rf-text">The gold florin is introduced</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1434</span><span class="rf-text">The Medici take power, ending the Albizzi's sway</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1498</span><span class="rf-text">Savonarola is hanged and burned in the Piazza della Signoria</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1569</span><span class="rf-text">Cosimo I is made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">Florence replaces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulla.jpg" alt="Sulla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Q._Pompeius_Rufus,_denarius,_54_BC,_RRC_434-1_(Sulla_only).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Florence lies in a basin ringed by hills, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, with three smaller rivers, the Mugnone, Ema and Greve, and some streams flowing through it. For much of the Quaternary the plain of Florence, Prato and Pistoia held a great lake, and ponds and marshes remained until the land was reclaimed in the 18th century. It is thought that a settlement stood at the confluence of the Mugnone and the Arno between the 10th and 8th centuries BCE. Between the 7th and 6th centuries BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> found and used a ford of the Arno near that confluence, and a bridge or ferry was probably built about ten metres from the present Ponte Vecchio. They preferred not to build on the plain, for reasons of defence, and settled on a hill about six kilometres away, at the fortified centre of Vipsul, today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiesole">Fiesole</a>.</p><p>Written history traditionally begins in 59 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded a village for army veterans, built in the style of an army camp. According to some stories Caesar wanted an outpost near Fiesole to watch the roads and communications. Some historians argue that a pre-Roman settlement existed, possibly destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>. The town was first named Fluentia, because it stood between two rivers, and the name later changed to Florentia, meaning the Flourishing Town. It lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-cassia">Via Cassia</a>, the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the north, and the Romans built ports on the Arno and the Mugnone.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michelangelo.jpg" alt="Michelangelo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michelangelo_Daniele_da_Volterra_(dettaglio).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Daniele da Volterra, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman settlement quickly became an important commercial centre. Around the military camp the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built an aqueduct from Monte Morello, a forum in what is now the Piazza della Repubblica, baths, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and an amphitheatre, and in 123 CE a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>. They organised the surrounding land by centuriation, and a nearby river port allowed trade up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>.</p><p>After the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Florence revived. In 1100 it was a commune, and its primary resource was the Arno, which gave power for industry, mainly textiles, and access to the sea for trade. Florentine merchant bankers brought financial innovations such as bills of exchange and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/double-entry-bookkeeping">double-entry bookkeeping</a> to medieval fairs. The gold florin, introduced in 1252, was the first European gold coin struck in sufficient quantities to play a significant commercial role since the 7th century, and it became the dominant trade coin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Florentine banks had branches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Florentine bankers financed the English kings during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. The city had 50,000 people in 1200, and by 1300 its population was 120,000, with a further 300,000 in the Contado. The 15th century, when its population was 60,000, was the age of the Medici and of the artists they patronised, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> and Botticelli.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odoacer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odoacer.jpg" alt="Odoacer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Odoacer_at_the_British_Museum_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">British Museum (photo), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Florence was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and Empire, and in 285 CE it became the capital of Tuscia. It was then held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, who alternated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in a struggle for the city, and from the 6th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered it in 774, and it became part of the March of Tuscany, centred on Lucca. Margrave Hugh chose Florence as his residence instead of Lucca around 1000. In 1115 the city came under an autonomous commune, the Republic of Florence.</p><p>After the Albizzi family held sway from 1382 to 1434, the Medici took power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> controlled the city from behind the scenes, and was followed by his son Piero and, in 1469, his grandson Lorenzo. Piero II was expelled in 1494 and a republic was restored. The Medici returned in 1512 with Spanish and papal troops, were driven out again in 1527, and came back in 1530 with the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a> became the first Duke of Florence, and Cosimo I was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> in 1569. The Medici reigned until Gian Gastone died in 1737.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis Stephen of Lorraine</a> then took the throne, and Tuscany passed to the Habsburg-Lorraine dynasty. From 1801 to 1807 Florence was the capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, and from 1808 to 1814 the prefecture of the French département of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>. The Habsburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> rulers returned and were deposed in 1859, and Tuscany joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861. Florence was its capital from 1865 to 1871.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella.jpg" alt="Giano della Bella" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella">Giano della Bella</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giano_della_Bella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Florence was long a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. After an anti-aristocratic movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella">Giano della Bella</a>, the Ordinances of Justice of 1293 shifted power from the aristocracy to the merchants and members of organised guilds. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> city the highest executive authority, the Signoria, was formed by drawing names from bags, one for each sixth of the city, every two months, a lottery that organised the political structure until 1434. Mobs were both common and influential, and families struggled constantly for power.</p><p>About 25,000 people were engaged in the wool industry. In 1338 there were about 17,000 beggars in the city, 4,000 of them on public relief, and six primary schools taught 10,000 pupils, including girls. Four high schools taught 600 students. The richest 10% of the population owned 68% of the wealth and the poorest 60% just 5%. The University of Florence was first founded in 1321.</p><p>Eastern merchants, some from the Oltrarno quarter, brought the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to Roman Florence, and the city was the seat of a bishopric from around the beginning of the 4th century. Most of its people later belonged to the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. In the 14th century the Florentine dialect, in the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, became the basis of a national literary language. A foreign community came to make up about one quarter of the population in the second half of the 19th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri.jpg" alt="Dante Alighieri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bargello_-_Kapelle_Fresko_2a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> fought over Florence, taking it by siege and losing it again, and by the 6th century the population had fallen to as few as 1,000, according to speculation. In the 13th century the city was divided by the struggle between the Ghibellines, supporters of the German emperor, and the pro-papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>, which began after the murder of the nobleman Buondelmonte del Buondelmonti. The Guelphs split into White and Black factions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-and-white-guelphs">White Guelphs</a> were exiled, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, who was sent into exile in 1301. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reduced the population by over half, and in 1378 the wool workers rose in the Revolt of the Ciompi.</p><p>On 26 April 1478 Jacopo de' Pazzi and his retainers tried to raise the city against the Medici, murdering Giuliano di Piero de' Medici and wounding his brother Lorenzo. On 23 May 1498 Savonarola was hanged and burned on the Piazza della Signoria and his ashes were dispersed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>. The ten-month siege of 1529 to 1530 ended the Republic and brought the destruction of the suburbs, the ruin of the export business and the confiscation of citizens' wealth.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Allied bombers struck central Florence on 25 September 1943, destroying many buildings and killing 215 civilians. In August 1944 the retreating Germans demolished the Arno's bridges except the Ponte Vecchio. Florence was liberated on 4 August 1944. On 4 November 1966 the Arno flooded the centre, killing at least 40 people and damaging millions of art treasures and rare books. A car bomb in 1993 killed five people behind the Uffizi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral.jpg" alt="Florence Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cattedrale_di_Santa_Maria_del_Fiore_%E2%80%93_Il_Duomo_di_Firenze.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Campbell-Hall, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Many academics consider Florence the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and Florentine artists and architects such as Brunelleschi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaccio">Masaccio</a>, Botticelli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> worked there. The cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Santa Maria del Fiore</a> was begun in 1296 to a design of Arnolfo di Cambio and structurally completed by 1436 with Brunelleschi's dome, which is still the largest brick dome of its kind in the world. Michelangelo's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> was made between 1501 and 1504. The Uffizi was founded on a large bequest from the last member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">Medici family</a>, and the church of San Lorenzo holds the Medici Chapels. Santa Croce is the burial place of Michelangelo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>.</p><p>Florence also left its language. The Florentine dialect became the base of standard Italian, through the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, Machiavelli and Guicciardini. Opera was invented in Florence in the late 16th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacopo-peri">Jacopo Peri</a>'s Dafne was first performed, and the piano was invented there in 1709 by Bartolomeo Cristofori. Two Medici were popes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> and Clement VII, and Catherine and Marie de' Medici became queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The centre is contained in medieval walls built in the 14th century, and the outline of the Roman city is still recognisable in its plan. The historic centre was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1982. Florence is today the capital of Tuscany, with a population of 361,625.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)<span>1275</span></a><a href="/songs/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Florence, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> in Tuscany, was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 59 BCE and became a wealthy commune of merchants and bankers. Under the Medici it was a birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> from 1865 to 1871.</em></p>

